From 8925d6355540392dc15bafaeb2a2a58582d84ca3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 19:40:41 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] Button prompts: a profile can draw the game's textures again
 as they load

A game's button symbols are often sprites in its own textures, so a
player with an Xbox, PlayStation or Nintendo pad sees the PSP's buttons.
A profile can now list textures by the keys Dump textures gives them
(GameProfile::edited_textures) and draw each again as it loads
(edit_texture): at any size, with the same texture coordinates, as a
texture pack's image is drawn. A texture pack's own image still wins.

What the drawing depends on is a TextureEditContext: the button prompts
to show and whether Texture filter is Sharp. The renderer works it out
each frame from the settings and the gamepad in use, and when it
changes, drops its cached textures so the listed ones are drawn again
the next time they are used. VulkanRenderer::texture_edit_context()
hands it to a profile's own screens. <prefix>_NO_TEXTURE_EDITS=1 leaves
the textures as they are.

A profile that sets button_prompts gets a Button prompts row on the
Controls page (input.button_prompts, <prefix>_BUTTON_PROMPTS):
Automatic, PSP, Xbox, PlayStation or Nintendo. Automatic takes the
pad's kind from SDL_GetGamepadType, a Steam Deck's own controls from its
name; with no pad it shows the PSP's. The face buttons follow the pad's
positions and the Confirm button setting (host/input/button_prompts.*),
covered by the input tests.

Tested with Dissidia 012 on Windows (clang build, USA disc): a scripted
Quick Battle in each style, and the owner playing with a DualSense and
DS4Windows' virtual DS4 and Xbox 360 pads, the setting and the pad
changed while playing. Not verified: a real Xbox, Nintendo or Steam
Deck controller, Linux, another game.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
